<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tel Dan stele</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">9th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century-bce">9th century BCE</a> · Also known as Tel Dan inscription</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tel-dan">Tel Dan</a> stele is a fragmentary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a> inscription of the 9th century BCE found in 1993–1994 at Tel Dan in northern Israel. Probably set up by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hazael">Hazael</a> of Aram-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, it boasts of victories over a king of Israel and a king of the '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-david">House of David</a>', the earliest widely accepted reference outside the Bible to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a>'s dynasty.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tel-dan-stele.jpg" alt="Tel Dan stele" width="320" height="309" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Oren Rozen · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:JRSLM_300116_Tel_Dan_Stele_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
